Universal Template:
```java
int left = 0;
for (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {
    // 1. Expand window: add nums[right] to state/frequency map
    state.add(nums[right]);

    // 2. Shrink window while constraint is violated (or to optimize)
    while (windowInvalid(state)) {
        state.remove(nums[left]);
        left++;
    }

    // 3. Update answer with valid window: [left, right]
    ans = Math.max(ans, right - left + 1);
}
```
• Complexity: O(N) time (each element enters and leaves window at most once), O(K) space for frequency hash table.

Mathematical Invariant:
`sum(i, j) = prefixSum[j] - prefixSum[i - 1] = k`
Rearranging: `prefixSum[i - 1] = prefixSum[j] - k`

Template:
```java
Map<Integer, Integer> prefixFreq = new HashMap<>();
prefixFreq.put(0, 1); // Base case for subarray starting at index 0
int currSum = 0, count = 0;
for (int x : nums) {
    currSum += x;
    count += prefixFreq.getOrDefault(currSum - k, 0);
    prefixFreq.put(currSum, prefixFreq.getOrDefault(currSum, 0) + 1);
}
```
• Edge cases: Zeroes, negative numbers (sliding window fails with negatives, but prefix sum + hash map works seamlessly!).

Applicability:
When problem asks for 'Minimize the maximum' or 'Maximize the minimum' (e.g. Koko Eating Bananas, Split Array Largest Sum, Capacity To Ship Packages Within D Days, Aggressive Cows).

Monotonic Invariant:
If condition `feasible(x)` is TRUE for capacity x, it is guaranteed TRUE for all `y > x` (Monotonic Predicate: F F F T T T).

Template:
```java
int low = minPossible, high = maxPossible, ans = high;
while (low <= high) {
    int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
    if (feasible(nums, mid, limit)) {
        ans = mid;      // Record possible answer
        high = mid - 1; // Try smaller capacity
    } else {
        low = mid + 1;  // Must increase capacity
    }
}
```
• Complexity: O(N * log(Range)).

Standard 3-Pointer In-Place Reversal:
```java
ListNode prev = null, curr = head;
while (curr != null) {
    ListNode next = curr.next;
    curr.next = prev;
    prev = curr;
    curr = next;
}
return prev;
```

For K-Group Reversal:
1. Count k nodes ahead. If < k nodes remain, leave them as is.
2. Keep `groupPrev` pointer immediately preceding the group to be reversed.
3. Reverse k nodes, connecting `groupPrev.next` to the new group head, and the old group head (now tail) to the next group's starting node.
4. Advance `groupPrev` to the reversed group's tail.

• General Binary Tree LCA (O(n) time, O(h) space):
```java
public TreeNode lowestCommonAncestor(TreeNode root, TreeNode p, TreeNode q) {
    if (root == null || root == p || root == q) return root;
    TreeNode left = lowestCommonAncestor(root.left, p, q);
    TreeNode right = lowestCommonAncestor(root.right, p, q);
    if (left != null && right != null) return root; // p and q in opposite subtrees
    return left != null ? left : right;
}
```

• Binary Search Tree (BST) LCA (O(h) time, O(1) space):
  - If both `p.val` and `q.val` < `root.val` → search `root.left`.
  - If both `p.val` and `q.val` > `root.val` → search `root.right`.
  - Else, current `root` is the split point (LCA)!

Template:
```java
void backtrack(int start, List<Integer> current, int[] nums) {
    result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
    for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
        // Prune duplicates (requires sorted nums!)
        if (i > start && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) continue;

        current.add(nums[i]);            // 1. Choose
        backtrack(i + 1, current, nums); // 2. Explore
        current.remove(current.size() - 1); // 3. Unchoose
    }
}
```
• Why `i > start`? It permits using identical duplicate values at different depth levels of the decision tree, but prevents starting sibling branches with the same value at the identical decision depth.

0/1 Knapsack (each item used at most once):
```java
int[] dp = new int[capacity + 1];
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    // Inner loop MUST run BACKWARDS from capacity down to weight[i]
    for (int w = capacity; w >= weights[i]; w--) {
        dp[w] = Math.max(dp[w], dp[w - weights[i]] + values[i]);
    }
}
```
• Why Reverse? If run forwards, `dp[w - weights[i]]` would have already been updated in the *current* iteration, accidentally allowing the item to be included multiple times (which is Unbounded Knapsack!). Backward traversal ensures `dp[w - weights[i]]` comes from previous step `i-1`.
